<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
  Towner (com.townwize.towner) — strip background location.

  Both flavours share src/main/AndroidManifest.xml, so the passenger app was
  shipping ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION for a code path it CANNOT REACH:

    - BackgroundGeolocation is referenced in exactly one file,
      src/pages/driver/LiveTracking/DriverLiveTracking.jsx. Passenger tracking
      uses foreground watchPosition and nothing else.
    - src/utils/appRole.js derives the role from the package id at runtime:
      com.townwize.wizer is the only id that becomes "driver". The Towner build
      is locked to passenger, and Login refuses a Wizer sign-in outright.

  Why it is worth removing rather than leaving harmlessly in place:

    1. Declaring ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION is what pulls an app into Google's
       background-location review — the prominent-disclosure declaration, a
       demo video, and a 2-6 week queue. The Wizer app genuinely needs that and
       will go through it. There is no reason for the Towner app to.
    2. Requesting a permission beyond an app's core functionality is itself a
       Play policy violation, so this was also a standing rejection risk.

  tools:node="remove" strips the element from the MERGED manifest, which means
  it also removes what the background-geolocation library contributes — the
  library declares the same permissions and service in its own manifest, which
  is why src/main needs tools:replace on the service at all.

  The service is removed too: without it the permissions would be meaningless,
  and leaving a location-typed foreground service declared in an app that never
  starts one invites exactly the questions we are avoiding.

  ⚠️ If a passenger-side feature ever needs background location, delete this
  file rather than working around it — and expect the review that follows.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!-- The background-geolocation service was the ONLY foreground service in
         the app, and the merge blame report confirms this permission is
         contributed by src/main alone — no library asks for it. With the
         service gone there is nothing left to start. -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <application>
        <service
            android:name="com.equimaps.capacitor_background_geolocation.BackgroundGeolocationService"
            tools:node="remove" />
    </application>
</manifest>
